  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">2026 Venezuela Earthquake</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The IFRC has launched an Emergency Appeal (MDRVE015) for CHF 50 million to respond to two major earthquakes of magnitude 7.2 and 7.5 that struck north-central Venezuela on 24 June 2026 in rapid succession. The appeal targets 300,000 people and covers immediate life-saving activities across shelter, livelihoods, multipurpose cash, health, WASH, psychosocial support, and protection, alongside strengthening of the Venezuelan Red Cross as the lead national actor.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The earthquakes struck with epicentres near Mor&#xf3;n and Yumare in Carabobo and Yaracuy states, west of Caracas, but their impact extended across much of the country. The operational focus of the appeal is concentrated in La Guaira, declared a Zona de Desastre, and Greater Caracas, with secondary coverage of Carabobo, Aragua, Miranda, and Falcon states as access and assessments permit.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The humanitarian need is acute and multidimensional: at least 188 deaths and 1,520 injuries have been reported, over 200 buildings have collapsed, and the health system is under extreme strain with pre-existing shortages compounded by a surge in trauma cases. The response is further complicated by macroeconomic fragility, infrastructure damage including the main airport, and the fact that the Venezuelan Red Cross headquarters itself sustained critical damage, limiting the National Society&apos;s autonomous capacity to scale up.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="3">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The primary beneficiaries are people directly affected by collapsed or damaged structures, those with injuries or missing relatives, households displaced or unable to return home, and communities experiencing disruptions to essential services. Particular attention is directed toward the approximately 530,000 people exposed to severe to extreme seismic intensity, as well as first responders and volunteers requiring psychosocial support.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The response is structured around the Venezuelan Red Cross&apos;s auxiliary role to public authorities, supported by IFRC Secretariat surge personnel, technical expertise, and the Panama Regional Logistics Unit as the supply chain hub. A CHF 2 million DREF loan was activated immediately to bridge the gap before the full appeal mobilises resources. Movement coordination is framed under the existing MCA for Venezuela and the Seville Agreement 2.0, while external coordination engages the Humanitarian Country Team, DG ECHO, AECID, and government ministries to avoid duplication.</narrative>
  </description>
